  "generated_text": " solve solve this problem, we can use a recursive approach. Let $f(n)$ be the number of good words of length $n$. We need to findff(7)$.\n\nWe can start by considering the base cases. For $n=1$, there are 3 good words: $A$, $B$, and $C$. So, $f(1)=3$.\n\nFor $n=2$, we can append any of the 3 letters to good good word of length 1, except for the letter that would violate the condition. So, $f(2)=3f(1)=3\\cdot3=9$.\n\nWe can continue this process to find $f(3)$, $f(4)$, and so on. We find thatff(3)=27$, $f(4)=81$, $f(5)=243$, $f(6)=729$, and $f(7)=2187$.\n\nTherefore, there are $\\boxed{2187}$ seven-letter good words.",
